This block issues one DB query per `recent_tool_id` to resolve `trigger_name`, which becomes an N+1 query pattern as the recent list grows. Prefer resolving all recent tool names in a single `WHERE Tool.id IN (...)` query and building the map in-memory.
```suggestion

        # Resolve recent_tool_ids -> trigger_name in one query to avoid N+1 lookups
        trigger_rows = db.execute(
            select(Tool.id, Tool.name).where(Tool.id.in_(recent_tool_ids))
        ).all()
        trigger_name_map: Dict[str, str] = {row.id: row.name for row in trigger_rows}

        for tool_id in recent_tool_ids:
            trigger_name = trigger_name_map.get(tool_id, tool_id)
```

`_build_strategy_results` loops over every enabled tool and executes multiple `SELECT count(*)` queries per tool (e.g., for trending/current/prev, popularity, success rate). This is an N+1 query pattern that will not meet the latency goals once the tool catalog grows. Consider aggregating counts with `GROUP BY tool_id` (and time buckets) and joining those results in-memory.
```suggestion
    # Batch aggregate counts for all tools to avoid per-tool queries
    tool_ids = [tool.id for tool in tools]
    current_counts: Dict[Any, int] = {}
    prev_counts: Dict[Any, int] = {}

    if tool_ids:
        current_rows = db.execute(
            select(ToolMetric.tool_id, func.count().label("count"))
            .where(
                ToolMetric.tool_id.in_(tool_ids),
                ToolMetric.timestamp >= week_ago,
            )
            .group_by(ToolMetric.tool_id)
        ).all()
        current_counts = {tool_id: count for tool_id, count in current_rows}

        prev_rows = db.execute(
            select(ToolMetric.tool_id, func.count().label("count"))
            .where(
                ToolMetric.tool_id.in_(tool_ids),
                ToolMetric.timestamp >= two_weeks_ago,
                ToolMetric.timestamp < week_ago,
            )
            .group_by(ToolMetric.tool_id)
        ).all()
        prev_counts = {tool_id: count for tool_id, count in prev_rows}

    for tool in tools:
        # Simple trending score based on execution count
        current = int(current_counts.get(tool.id, 0))
        prev = int(prev_counts.get(tool.id, 0))
```
